WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.458
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:12.090
JAMES BLAIR: So the goal
here is to just walk through

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:17.040
like a really simple use case
of plumbertableau to highlight

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.890
a couple of things-- one,
how it relates to Plumber

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:23.070
and how it extends what
Plumber can already do.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:25.920
And then the other piece
here is to just showcase

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:30.510
what is plumbertableau trying
to solve from the developer

00:00:30.510 --> 00:00:31.710
perspective.

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:35.010
So as an R user, what do
I get out of this tool?

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:38.670
And then the other side is as
a Tableau developer or Tableau

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:41.730
user, how do I leverage
something that's been developed

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:44.340
in plumbertableau?

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:48.210
What's the workflow, and what's
the pattern for using this

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:49.270
once it's been developed?

00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:52.510
So that's the goal here.

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:54.600
I think there might be a
few assumptions about--

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:56.550
prerequisite Plumber
knowledge, but I'll

00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:59.430
try to highlight some
of the main ideas

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:00.930
if there's some
unfamiliarity there.

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:03.750
But just like I said, the use
case here is really simple,

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:06.570
but I think it will
help us ignore--

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:08.070
or not necessarily
ignore, but it'll

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:09.487
give us something
concrete that we

00:01:09.487 --> 00:01:12.300
can work with that's not overly
complex so that we can focus

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:15.122
instead on how the tool is
working rather than what we're

00:01:15.122 --> 00:01:16.330
trying to accomplish with it.

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:19.170
So all that being
said, the idea is

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:23.040
I want to capitalize some text--

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:24.090
super easy.

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:27.180
But and in R, I could do
that with just the toupper

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:28.993
function that's already
there, but to make

00:01:28.993 --> 00:01:30.660
this a little bit
easier to think about,

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:33.300
we're going to create our
own function called caps,

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:37.560
which takes some input text.

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:40.500
And then we're going
to use toupper in here

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:42.270
on our input text.

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:44.610
So again, nothing
crazy, but the idea

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:46.800
is we can give this
some sort of string,

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.830
"HELLO WORLD,"
and we'll get back

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:51.210
the capitalized form of that.

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:53.880
Now, let's say that
for some reason

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:57.090
this functionality is super
critical to some Tableau

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:58.170
document that I have.

00:01:58.170 --> 00:01:59.610
I have a bunch of
text, and it has

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:01.345
to be capitalized
for my executives,

00:02:01.345 --> 00:02:02.970
and this is the way
I'm going to do it.

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:06.298
Now, it's a stretch,
but that's the scenario

00:02:06.298 --> 00:02:07.340
we're going to work with.

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:11.340
So the idea is this function
right now just lives in R,

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:13.980
and what I really
want is first, I

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:17.112
want to make this accessible
to other tools generically,

00:02:17.112 --> 00:02:18.570
and then we'll
worry about Tableau.

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:22.440
So one of the ways to do that
is to use the Plumber package

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:26.200
and to turn this function that
we've defined into an API.

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:30.200
So I'm going to
bring in Plumber now.

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:34.130
And then Plummer works
by using special comments

00:02:34.130 --> 00:02:37.640
to change existing R functions,
like what we have written here,

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:41.420
into API endpoints that
can respond to common HTTP

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:42.440
requests.

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:44.690
So it's nice in the sense
that I don't necessarily

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:47.270
have to write a bunch of new
code to turn this into an API.

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:49.517
I just have to add some
new comments to do that.

00:02:49.517 --> 00:02:50.600
So we're going to come in.

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:53.060
And the special
signifier for Plumber

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:56.450
is the standard comment symbol
followed by an asterisk,

00:02:56.450 --> 00:02:59.090
and then there are
these decorators

00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:01.310
that begin with the
@ symbol that we

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:04.627
can use to define different
pieces and components of what

00:03:04.627 --> 00:03:06.710
we want Plumber to do and
how we want it to handle

00:03:06.710 --> 00:03:07.710
the functions we define.

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:09.680
So I'm going to create
an API title here.

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:14.870
We'll call this "Simple
capitalization example."

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:16.220
We'll give it a description--

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:21.610
"Capitalize some text."

00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:24.130
So this is just a generic
title and description

00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:26.290
for the API and collection
of different endpoints.

00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:28.930
In our case, we're only
building this one function,

00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:29.930
this caps function.

00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:32.138
So I'm going to create a
little description for this.

00:03:32.138 --> 00:03:37.750
This is "Capitalize input
text and return the result."

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:39.672
And then here's the
significant part.

00:03:39.672 --> 00:03:40.630
Here's where I define--

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:43.420
OK, we're going to
listen for POST requests

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:46.780
at the "capitalize" path.

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:49.480
So here's the significant
part where I tell

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:52.540
Plumber, look, respond to
these types of requests

00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:53.860
at this location.

00:03:53.860 --> 00:03:57.730
And whenever a request comes
in that matches that criteria,

00:03:57.730 --> 00:04:00.190
this function will be
executed with the contents

00:04:00.190 --> 00:04:02.478
of that request on a
very simplified way.

00:04:02.478 --> 00:04:04.270
So I'm going to just
go ahead and save this

00:04:04.270 --> 00:04:08.200
here so that we can use some
of the RStudio niceties.

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:10.670
So now, once I've saved
this as a Plumber file,

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:13.880
you'll see that RStudio
gives me this Run API option.

00:04:13.880 --> 00:04:18.310
And if I select this here, we
get this cool little interface

00:04:18.310 --> 00:04:20.500
that pops up in our viewer pane.

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:24.490
And this is a UI,
user interface,

00:04:24.490 --> 00:04:26.080
that's been
generated dynamically

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:30.160
by Plumber in combination with
Swagger, which is the rendering

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:31.340
tool that's been used here.

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:34.355
And this shows us, here's our
title, here's our description.

00:04:34.355 --> 00:04:35.980
And if I change the
values where we see

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:38.140
"Capitalize some text"
is what we see over here.

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:40.810
And if I went back and
changed that and we ran this,

00:04:40.810 --> 00:04:42.347
we'd see the change
updated here.

00:04:42.347 --> 00:04:43.930
And if I come down
here to capitalize,

00:04:43.930 --> 00:04:45.310
we can expand this out.

00:04:45.310 --> 00:04:47.440
We see our input text here.

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:49.810
We see some information
about the different types

00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:52.150
of responses we might get
back, and I can actually

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:55.060
go ahead and try this out,
and we can say "hello world,"

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:58.040
and we can hit Execute,
and there we go.

00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:00.010
We have our response
back capitalized

00:05:00.010 --> 00:05:03.190
form of what the input
that we provided, awesome.

00:05:03.190 --> 00:05:07.690
Now, at this point,
we've done 95%

00:05:07.690 --> 00:05:11.230
of the work necessary to get
this to work inside of Tableau.

00:05:11.230 --> 00:05:14.530
And in fact, if we just take
a step back and ignore Tableau

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:18.130
for a second and think about
what we've done more broadly,

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:20.170
we've taken this
function that we wrote,

00:05:20.170 --> 00:05:22.255
the "Capitalize this
text," and we've

00:05:22.255 --> 00:05:24.700
turned it into this
service that can be used,

00:05:24.700 --> 00:05:26.770
regardless of the
tool that's using it.

00:05:26.770 --> 00:05:29.800
Like I could-- somebody writing
Python, or somebody writing Go,

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:31.450
or somebody writing
C++, or Java,

00:05:31.450 --> 00:05:34.730
or whatever could call out
to this service and say, hey,

00:05:34.730 --> 00:05:37.683
here's some text to capitalize,
and then R would execute what

00:05:37.683 --> 00:05:39.850
it needs to do and return
back the capitalized text.

00:05:39.850 --> 00:05:42.220
So it made this very generic
interface to function

00:05:42.220 --> 00:05:44.590
but which can be
super, super useful.

00:05:44.590 --> 00:05:48.430
Now, at this point, if I want
to get this to Tableau, I have--

00:05:48.430 --> 00:05:50.890
into Tableau, there's a
couple of routes I could go.

00:05:50.890 --> 00:05:53.200
One is I could
start figuring out

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:55.480
how exactly Tableau
wants to communicate

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:56.500
with external services.

00:05:56.500 --> 00:05:59.020
Tableau has a very
specific way of sending

00:05:59.020 --> 00:06:02.575
requests and a very specific way
in which it expects responses.

00:06:02.575 --> 00:06:04.450
And I could go through,
and I could build out

00:06:04.450 --> 00:06:08.320
an API that meets
that criteria or meets

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:11.320
the contract that Tableau
expects an API to be.

00:06:11.320 --> 00:06:14.560
Or I could use the
plumbertableau package, which

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:16.280
does all of that work for me.

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:19.480
And so I'm going to go ahead
and bring in plumbertableau now,

00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:21.910
which, as the name
suggests, is a package that

00:06:21.910 --> 00:06:24.280
expands the
functionality of Plumber

00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:27.970
to make it easy to
build APIs essentially

00:06:27.970 --> 00:06:32.230
that operate as analytics
extensions inside of Tableau.

00:06:32.230 --> 00:06:34.510
So once I bring the
plumbertableau package in,

00:06:34.510 --> 00:06:37.070
there's two things
that I now need to do.

00:06:37.070 --> 00:06:39.490
One is there's a couple
of new annotations

00:06:39.490 --> 00:06:42.550
that I need to provide when
I describe my function.

00:06:42.550 --> 00:06:50.270
One is @tableauArg, and
then one is @tableauReturn.

00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:53.290
And as you can probably
guess from the naming,

00:06:53.290 --> 00:06:56.260
@tableauReturn is where
we define the arguments

00:06:56.260 --> 00:06:57.940
that we expect Tableau
to be providing--

00:06:57.940 --> 00:07:00.268
in this case, that's
this input text argument.

00:07:00.268 --> 00:07:01.810
And we might have
multiple arguments.

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:05.420
We could have input text
one and input text two,

00:07:05.420 --> 00:07:07.120
or we can have different things.

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:08.600
Maybe there's
parameters-- again,

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:10.540
this is a very simple
use case, but perhaps I

00:07:10.540 --> 00:07:14.440
have a previously trained
machine learning model,

00:07:14.440 --> 00:07:16.990
and I'm expecting Tableau
to submit new data,

00:07:16.990 --> 00:07:19.360
and then I'm going to run
that new data through my model

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:21.190
and generate
predictions and then

00:07:21.190 --> 00:07:23.860
send the predicted
outcomes back to Tableau

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:25.550
for further analysis.

00:07:25.550 --> 00:07:29.195
In which case, I might have a
dozen or more different inputs

00:07:29.195 --> 00:07:31.570
that I expect to come from
Tableau, and each one of those

00:07:31.570 --> 00:07:35.920
can be accompanied by this
@tableauReturn specification

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:39.267
here that allows me to
define some information

00:07:39.267 --> 00:07:40.100
about that argument.

00:07:40.100 --> 00:07:41.475
So here, I'm going
to say this is

00:07:41.475 --> 00:07:45.730
input_text is the name of the
argument, and its type is--

00:07:45.730 --> 00:07:46.780
if I could spell.

00:07:46.780 --> 00:07:49.090
Its type is character.

00:07:49.090 --> 00:07:50.590
And then here's a
brief description.

00:07:50.590 --> 00:07:55.960
This is the text we
want to capitalized.

00:07:55.960 --> 00:07:56.950
Easy enough?

00:07:56.950 --> 00:08:00.370
@tableauReturn is what is
my extension sending back

00:08:00.370 --> 00:08:01.690
to Tableau?

00:08:01.690 --> 00:08:04.337
I first need to define what
data type I'm sending back.

00:08:04.337 --> 00:08:05.920
So again, I'm going
to be sending back

00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:13.475
a character, value, and this
is the capitalized input text.

00:08:13.475 --> 00:08:15.850
So those two new things--
@tableauArg and @tableauReturn.

00:08:15.850 --> 00:08:18.400
And the last piece here is I
need to add this modifier down

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:20.830
at the bottom, and
a modifier is simply

00:08:20.830 --> 00:08:24.470
a function that makes changes
to an existing Plumber router.

00:08:24.470 --> 00:08:27.220
So here, the way to do
that in this context

00:08:27.220 --> 00:08:29.620
is to use this
Plumber decorator,

00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:34.330
and then we're going to
say tableau_extension.

00:08:34.330 --> 00:08:35.929
And this is a
function-- in fact,

00:08:35.929 --> 00:08:37.721
if we can just look at
this really quickly.

00:08:37.721 --> 00:08:40.390
This is a function that is
exported from plumbertableau.

00:08:40.390 --> 00:08:42.723
It's one of the only functions
that the package actually

00:08:42.723 --> 00:08:43.809
exports.

00:08:43.809 --> 00:08:46.010
And this-- there's
an example here

00:08:46.010 --> 00:08:48.640
of how you might use that in the
way that we're using it here.

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:50.320
But essentially,
this function does

00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:53.620
all the necessary modification
to the underlying API

00:08:53.620 --> 00:08:55.540
so that it matches
and behaves the way

00:08:55.540 --> 00:08:56.950
that Tableau wants it to.

00:08:56.950 --> 00:09:00.400
And the advantage here is this
means that as the R developer,

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:03.100
I don't need to necessarily
worry about how Tableau

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:04.930
is communicating with my API.

00:09:04.930 --> 00:09:07.360
I can trust that the API will
communicate appropriately,

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:11.200
as long as I use this modifier
once I've defined everything

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:14.290
that I want to
define within my API.

00:09:14.290 --> 00:09:16.583
OK, so I'm going
to save the file.

00:09:16.583 --> 00:09:18.000
Again, just to
review-- we brought

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:19.350
in the plumbertableau package.

00:09:19.350 --> 00:09:21.480
We added @tableauArg
and @tableauReturn,

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:24.450
and we modified the API
with this tableau_extension

00:09:24.450 --> 00:09:25.207
function.

00:09:25.207 --> 00:09:26.790
You might notice
that I'm not actually

00:09:26.790 --> 00:09:29.670
executing the function,
and that's just

00:09:29.670 --> 00:09:32.470
the semantic of Plumber itself.

00:09:32.470 --> 00:09:35.875
So here is the function name
or the function reference.

00:09:35.875 --> 00:09:37.500
I'm not executing
the function, but I'm

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:41.010
indicating call this
function on the API

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:44.220
that's previously defined and
allow that function to modify

00:09:44.220 --> 00:09:47.590
the API as it needs to.

00:09:47.590 --> 00:09:51.150
With these changes in place,
if I run the API again,

00:09:51.150 --> 00:09:53.550
I see basically the
same page, but there's

00:09:53.550 --> 00:09:55.100
a few subtle differences.

00:09:55.100 --> 00:09:56.790
One is these new
links show up here.

00:09:56.790 --> 00:09:58.170
These are
automatically generated

00:09:58.170 --> 00:10:01.020
as part of this
plumbertableau package,

00:10:01.020 --> 00:10:03.240
and we won't spend time
looking at them here,

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:07.050
but we will in a moment once
we transition to deployment.

00:10:07.050 --> 00:10:10.530
But if I scroll down and open up
my capitalized endpoint again,

00:10:10.530 --> 00:10:12.940
now, things look very different.

00:10:12.940 --> 00:10:16.680
And the reason is because one of
the things that plumbertableau

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:19.770
does is it automatically
will create and generate

00:10:19.770 --> 00:10:22.950
documentation about how
Tableau is going to interact

00:10:22.950 --> 00:10:26.010
with this particular extension
as well as an example

00:10:26.010 --> 00:10:28.240
request of what Tableau
is going to submit.

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:32.520
So here, this request that
we see here is in the format

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:34.950
that Tableau will be
submitting requests.

00:10:34.950 --> 00:10:38.730
Tableau submits a JSON object
with two nested values, script,

00:10:38.730 --> 00:10:41.550
and data, and so we see
those referenced here,

00:10:41.550 --> 00:10:44.130
and then the data object
contains the actual data

00:10:44.130 --> 00:10:46.710
values that are being
passed over from Tableau.

00:10:46.710 --> 00:10:50.430
If we inspect this, we can see
that arg1 is the first value

00:10:50.430 --> 00:10:54.030
here, and if we look
just above this,

00:10:54.030 --> 00:10:58.920
this table here shows a mapping
between what my function in R

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:01.870
has defined and what
Tableau is going to submit.

00:11:01.870 --> 00:11:05.430
So we can see that input text
in my R function corresponds

00:11:05.430 --> 00:11:08.880
to arg1 in this
Tableau data object.

00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:11.310
It's a character type
value, and here's

00:11:11.310 --> 00:11:14.280
a description of what that
value is, and it's not optional.

00:11:14.280 --> 00:11:16.040
It's a required field.

00:11:16.040 --> 00:11:18.270
I can still try this out,
so I can click Try It Out.

00:11:18.270 --> 00:11:21.440
I can come in here and
change the value of arg1

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:24.820
to "hello everyone."

00:11:24.820 --> 00:11:26.570
Execute this, and
if we scroll down,

00:11:26.570 --> 00:11:29.710
we see the capitalized
version of that text being

00:11:29.710 --> 00:11:32.500
returned back.

00:11:32.500 --> 00:11:35.588
Now, I'm going to pause
there and highlight

00:11:35.588 --> 00:11:36.380
a couple of things.

00:11:36.380 --> 00:11:42.500
One is as the R developer here,
I haven't touched Tableau.

00:11:42.500 --> 00:11:43.690
I haven't opened Tableau.

00:11:43.690 --> 00:11:45.277
I haven't needed
to look at Tableau.

00:11:45.277 --> 00:11:47.110
I haven't needed to
visit Tableau's website.

00:11:47.110 --> 00:11:51.310
But regardless of that, I've
been able to build an extension

00:11:51.310 --> 00:11:54.430
and verified that it
will work with Tableau,

00:11:54.430 --> 00:11:57.430
because I have an example of
what a Tableau request will

00:11:57.430 --> 00:11:57.990
look like.

00:11:57.990 --> 00:11:59.650
Let me just open this
up one more time.

00:11:59.650 --> 00:12:02.890
When I click Try It Out in here
and run this request, and say,

00:12:02.890 --> 00:12:07.030
"this is a test,"
this is me verifying,

00:12:07.030 --> 00:12:10.195
yep, this extension seems to
be doing exactly what I expect

00:12:10.195 --> 00:12:12.130
it to be doing, and
I have confidence

00:12:12.130 --> 00:12:16.030
that it will behave that way
when it's connected to Tableau.

00:12:16.030 --> 00:12:18.435
So the advantage is I can
do all of this within a tool

00:12:18.435 --> 00:12:20.060
that I'm comfortable
and familiar with.

00:12:20.060 --> 00:12:21.880
In this case, I'm using
RStudio Workbench.

00:12:21.880 --> 00:12:23.612
I might be using
RStudio on my desktop.

00:12:23.612 --> 00:12:24.820
I might not be using RStudio.

00:12:24.820 --> 00:12:27.538
I might be developing
this in some other editor

00:12:27.538 --> 00:12:28.330
or some other form.

00:12:28.330 --> 00:12:31.420
But regardless, I have
access to this interface,

00:12:31.420 --> 00:12:33.460
either directly in
RStudio or in a browser,

00:12:33.460 --> 00:12:37.390
and I can test to verify
the behavior of my extension

00:12:37.390 --> 00:12:40.210
without needing to either
open Tableau myself

00:12:40.210 --> 00:12:42.190
or rely on somebody
that has Tableau access,

00:12:42.190 --> 00:12:44.160
and that was one of our goals.

00:12:44.160 --> 00:12:45.790
If I take a step
back and just look

00:12:45.790 --> 00:12:48.130
at some of the philosophies
that went into designing

00:12:48.130 --> 00:12:50.530
plumbertableau, part
of this was to say

00:12:50.530 --> 00:12:56.760
we want to make things easy
for both populations here.

00:12:56.760 --> 00:12:58.340
One is the developers.

00:12:58.340 --> 00:13:00.040
How do we make
things easy for them?

00:13:00.040 --> 00:13:01.990
We build and extend tools
that they're already

00:13:01.990 --> 00:13:03.490
comfortable and
familiar with, which

00:13:03.490 --> 00:13:08.290
is why we chose to extend
Plumber and use that framework.

00:13:08.290 --> 00:13:11.107
We also make it easy for them
to test, validate, and confirm

00:13:11.107 --> 00:13:12.690
the behavior of
things without needing

00:13:12.690 --> 00:13:16.140
to rely on other tools, which
is what we just observed.

00:13:16.140 --> 00:13:20.200
The other side of this is how
do we help the Tableau user?

00:13:20.200 --> 00:13:21.890
They might not know
anything about R.

00:13:21.890 --> 00:13:23.640
They might not know
anything about Plumber

00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:25.020
or any of these tools.

00:13:25.020 --> 00:13:27.990
They just are building Tableau
workbooks and dashboards

00:13:27.990 --> 00:13:29.940
and visualizations,
and they want access

00:13:29.940 --> 00:13:32.160
to the things that
have been built here.

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:34.920
Now, in some organizations,
the R developer and the Tableau

00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:36.540
developer might
be the same person

00:13:36.540 --> 00:13:38.328
or might work together
on the same team.

00:13:38.328 --> 00:13:39.870
In other organizations,
they may have

00:13:39.870 --> 00:13:41.528
no idea who one another are.

00:13:41.528 --> 00:13:44.070
It might be two totally separate
groups, two totally separate

00:13:44.070 --> 00:13:45.270
individuals.

00:13:45.270 --> 00:13:47.040
And we wanted that to--

00:13:47.040 --> 00:13:49.470
we wanted to support
that type of distinction

00:13:49.470 --> 00:13:52.020
between these two roles.

00:13:52.020 --> 00:13:56.910
OK, now, at this point, I have
a fully functioning running

00:13:56.910 --> 00:13:59.625
Tableau extension
that I've built in R.

00:13:59.625 --> 00:14:03.610
The problem is Tableau cannot
access it at this point.

00:14:03.610 --> 00:14:05.310
Now, if I was running--

00:14:05.310 --> 00:14:08.550
say, for example, I was
running RStudio Desktop

00:14:08.550 --> 00:14:10.080
and I had to have
[? R ?] Desktop,

00:14:10.080 --> 00:14:11.640
I could stitch these
things together.

00:14:11.640 --> 00:14:13.847
I could run the API
inside of RStudio Desktop.

00:14:13.847 --> 00:14:15.180
I could open up Tableau Desktop.

00:14:15.180 --> 00:14:16.840
I could configure them
to speak to one another,

00:14:16.840 --> 00:14:17.840
and that would be great.

00:14:17.840 --> 00:14:19.275
But that's a very
limited utility,

00:14:19.275 --> 00:14:21.150
because it's still just--
everything is still

00:14:21.150 --> 00:14:21.650
local to me.

00:14:21.650 --> 00:14:23.358
Everything's still
running on my desktop,

00:14:23.358 --> 00:14:25.740
and I'm not going to pass my
laptop around the department

00:14:25.740 --> 00:14:27.420
to give people access
to my dashboard.

00:14:27.420 --> 00:14:30.473
I need some sort of
centralized hosting mechanism.

00:14:30.473 --> 00:14:31.890
On the Tableau
side, that's either

00:14:31.890 --> 00:14:34.320
going to be Tableau
online or Tableau server.

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:38.940
On the R side, the easiest
way to distribute and share

00:14:38.940 --> 00:14:41.220
and provide access
to this extension

00:14:41.220 --> 00:14:43.890
is through RStudio Connect.

00:14:43.890 --> 00:14:45.990
And RStudio Connect is
a publishing platform

00:14:45.990 --> 00:14:49.230
that supports publishing all
kinds of different R and Python

00:14:49.230 --> 00:14:52.410
content, including these
Tableau extensions like the one

00:14:52.410 --> 00:14:53.590
that we've built here.

00:14:53.590 --> 00:14:56.340
And in order to publish
this to RStudio Connect,

00:14:56.340 --> 00:14:59.635
I can select this little
publish button here inside

00:14:59.635 --> 00:15:01.260
of the RStudio
development environment.

00:15:01.260 --> 00:15:05.730
I can click Publish API,
and then select details

00:15:05.730 --> 00:15:07.860
about where I want to
publish it, the name of it,

00:15:07.860 --> 00:15:08.847
things like that.

00:15:08.847 --> 00:15:10.680
Now, I've already gone
through and published

00:15:10.680 --> 00:15:12.790
this API or one
very similar to it,

00:15:12.790 --> 00:15:14.610
so I'm going to
just open that now.

00:15:14.610 --> 00:15:17.220
So I'm going to come
into RStudio Connect.

00:15:17.220 --> 00:15:20.400
Let me navigate to
this particular API.

00:15:20.400 --> 00:15:24.240
And then we'll notice there's
a few subtle differences here

00:15:24.240 --> 00:15:26.910
in what we look at
in terms of the title

00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:30.120
and description of the API,
but the behavior and everything

00:15:30.120 --> 00:15:32.520
like that is the same.

00:15:32.520 --> 00:15:37.492
So this is that same API that
we were just working with, now

00:15:37.492 --> 00:15:38.700
published on RStudio Connect.

00:15:38.700 --> 00:15:40.533
And something that
you'll immediately notice

00:15:40.533 --> 00:15:43.290
is this looks very different
than what we saw before.

00:15:43.290 --> 00:15:45.420
When we ran this
inside of RStudio,

00:15:45.420 --> 00:15:47.730
we saw an interface
that looked like this.

00:15:47.730 --> 00:15:51.125
If you've done any sort of API
development or API work before,

00:15:51.125 --> 00:15:53.250
you're likely familiar with
this type of interface.

00:15:53.250 --> 00:15:56.880
It's a very common way of
exposing the functionality

00:15:56.880 --> 00:15:58.650
of an API as well as
providing mechanisms

00:15:58.650 --> 00:16:00.550
for testing and
validating the API.

00:16:00.550 --> 00:16:04.080
And so this is a very
developer-centric view

00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:06.640
of what I've built.

00:16:06.640 --> 00:16:08.290
On the other side,
once we publish this

00:16:08.290 --> 00:16:10.870
to RStudio Connect, we're now
switching gears and saying,

00:16:10.870 --> 00:16:13.870
OK, now we're not necessarily
trying to give the developer

00:16:13.870 --> 00:16:16.690
tools anymore, but
rather we're trying

00:16:16.690 --> 00:16:21.070
to expose this in a way that's
intuitive for the Tableau user

00:16:21.070 --> 00:16:22.510
to know what they need to do.

00:16:22.510 --> 00:16:25.810
And they don't need to know
how the API request is formed.

00:16:25.810 --> 00:16:28.810
They don't need to know
how the endpoint responds.

00:16:28.810 --> 00:16:32.380
What they need to know is
how do I use this extension

00:16:32.380 --> 00:16:34.840
from Tableau.

00:16:34.840 --> 00:16:36.610
And so if we look at
this document that's

00:16:36.610 --> 00:16:38.470
been pulled up here
on RStudio Connect,

00:16:38.470 --> 00:16:40.930
we could see that's exactly
what this is trying to provide.

00:16:40.930 --> 00:16:43.120
It gives the same
title description

00:16:43.120 --> 00:16:46.900
that we saw previously, and then
it has this usage section here

00:16:46.900 --> 00:16:48.160
that defines--

00:16:48.160 --> 00:16:50.110
it's a literal Copy and Paste.

00:16:50.110 --> 00:16:51.940
Here's how you use this
inside of Tableau--

00:16:51.940 --> 00:16:55.330
copy this script function
into your Tableau workbook

00:16:55.330 --> 00:16:56.950
under a calculated field.

00:16:56.950 --> 00:16:59.298
Make a change to the
input value to match

00:16:59.298 --> 00:17:01.090
what you're trying to
provide from Tableau,

00:17:01.090 --> 00:17:02.262
and you're good to go.

00:17:02.262 --> 00:17:04.220
And we'll take a look at
that in just a moment.

00:17:04.220 --> 00:17:05.845
If we scroll down a
little bit further,

00:17:05.845 --> 00:17:08.260
we see additional information
about the arguments

00:17:08.260 --> 00:17:10.569
that we're expecting
Tableau to provide

00:17:10.569 --> 00:17:12.572
as well as the return
value that we're going

00:17:12.572 --> 00:17:13.780
to be giving back to Tableau.

00:17:13.780 --> 00:17:15.550
And again, all of
this is being powered

00:17:15.550 --> 00:17:17.710
by those additional
annotations that we

00:17:17.710 --> 00:17:22.150
added when we introduced
plumbertableau to our API.

00:17:22.150 --> 00:17:24.819
One thing that I'll note is
that if we come back just

00:17:24.819 --> 00:17:26.829
for a moment to our
development environment

00:17:26.829 --> 00:17:29.770
and take a look, when we
defined Tableau return,

00:17:29.770 --> 00:17:31.750
we not only described
what we're returning

00:17:31.750 --> 00:17:33.910
but we were very specific
about the type of data

00:17:33.910 --> 00:17:35.440
that we're going
to be returning.

00:17:35.440 --> 00:17:38.260
And the reason for that is
because if we come back now

00:17:38.260 --> 00:17:41.200
to RStudio Connect, you'll see
that the Script function is

00:17:41.200 --> 00:17:42.640
strongly typed.

00:17:42.640 --> 00:17:45.220
This is a Script function,
and then this STR

00:17:45.220 --> 00:17:46.840
is a reference to
the fact that we

00:17:46.840 --> 00:17:51.450
expect a string value to
come back from the extension.

00:17:51.450 --> 00:17:54.540
So because we
provided information

00:17:54.540 --> 00:17:56.983
when we developed
this that said, hey,

00:17:56.983 --> 00:17:58.650
we're going to return
a character value,

00:17:58.650 --> 00:18:01.050
we know when we generate
this documentation

00:18:01.050 --> 00:18:03.870
that we should put script
string as the Script String

00:18:03.870 --> 00:18:06.315
function using this
particular extension.

00:18:06.315 --> 00:18:08.190
And this again, the idea
is just convenience.

00:18:08.190 --> 00:18:10.773
We want to make this as easy as
possible for the Tableau user,

00:18:10.773 --> 00:18:13.290
and then we come in,
Copy, and Paste this

00:18:13.290 --> 00:18:14.600
into their Tableau workbook.

00:18:14.600 --> 00:18:15.708
So let's do that.

00:18:15.708 --> 00:18:16.750
Let's see this in action.

00:18:16.750 --> 00:18:19.950
I'm going to grab this
script that we have here.

00:18:19.950 --> 00:18:24.383
We're going to open up a new
workbook on Tableau Online.

00:18:24.383 --> 00:18:25.300
I'm going to zoom out.

00:18:25.300 --> 00:18:26.842
I've got to connect
to a data source,

00:18:26.842 --> 00:18:28.620
so we'll just connect
to the default one.

00:18:28.620 --> 00:18:30.800
I'm going to zoom
in here a little bit

00:18:30.800 --> 00:18:33.160
just so we can see
a little bit better.

00:18:33.160 --> 00:18:35.140
First thing I'm going
to do is just save this,

00:18:35.140 --> 00:18:37.350
because in order to use
analytics extensions

00:18:37.350 --> 00:18:39.850
we have to have
a saved workbook.

00:18:39.850 --> 00:18:41.500
Go ahead and save that.

00:18:41.500 --> 00:18:46.590
And then I'm going to
now create a parameter.

00:18:46.590 --> 00:18:51.090
We'll call this Input Text, and
this just gives us something

00:18:51.090 --> 00:18:53.000
that we can in real
time work with and see.

00:18:53.000 --> 00:18:54.667
So we're going to
save this is a string,

00:18:54.667 --> 00:18:57.600
and the current value
is "hello world."

00:18:57.600 --> 00:19:01.320
Go ahead and hit OK
there, and then let's

00:19:01.320 --> 00:19:03.490
actually show this parameter.

00:19:03.490 --> 00:19:04.990
So over here on the
right-hand side,

00:19:04.990 --> 00:19:07.890
we have this input text field,
and we can use this field

00:19:07.890 --> 00:19:11.130
to input anything and it's
referenceable from our Tableau

00:19:11.130 --> 00:19:11.880
workbook.

00:19:11.880 --> 00:19:13.800
And the significance
now is we can now

00:19:13.800 --> 00:19:16.320
go through the process of
creating a calculated field.

00:19:16.320 --> 00:19:18.330
We'll call this caps,
and here's where

00:19:18.330 --> 00:19:21.780
we're going to paste in that
Script function that we've

00:19:21.780 --> 00:19:23.130
copied from RStudio Connect.

00:19:23.130 --> 00:19:25.290
And you'll notice the
first argument here

00:19:25.290 --> 00:19:28.935
is the location of this
extension on RStudio Connect.

00:19:28.935 --> 00:19:31.170
And so this is the
path to the extension

00:19:31.170 --> 00:19:33.690
as hosted on RStudio
Connect, and then the second

00:19:33.690 --> 00:19:35.957
and any subsequent
arguments are the values--

00:19:35.957 --> 00:19:38.040
are a reference to the
values that we want to pass

00:19:38.040 --> 00:19:40.690
from Tableau to the extension.

00:19:40.690 --> 00:19:44.310
So I'm going to replace string
value with Input Text, which

00:19:44.310 --> 00:19:47.220
is a reference to our parameter
that we created, and then hit

00:19:47.220 --> 00:19:48.970
OK.

00:19:48.970 --> 00:19:51.240
And now, we're going to
bring this calculated field

00:19:51.240 --> 00:19:53.510
into our workbook, so it'll
take a moment to load.

00:19:53.510 --> 00:19:55.260
And then we're going
to-- it automatically

00:19:55.260 --> 00:19:58.110
defaults to a color value,
but we don't want that.

00:19:58.110 --> 00:20:00.360
We'll call this-- we'll
change this to be a label.

00:20:00.360 --> 00:20:01.752
And here, we can see--

00:20:01.752 --> 00:20:03.960
there's a way to expand it,
and I never remember how.

00:20:03.960 --> 00:20:07.770
But if we hover, we can see
the capitalized rendered text

00:20:07.770 --> 00:20:09.062
of "hello world."

00:20:09.062 --> 00:20:11.520
And just to showcase that it
doesn't just do "hello world,"

00:20:11.520 --> 00:20:17.570
we can come over and say, "this
is an analytics extension."

00:20:17.570 --> 00:20:21.460
And if we hover again, we see
this is an analytics extension

00:20:21.460 --> 00:20:24.760
in full capitalization.

00:20:24.760 --> 00:20:26.620
So we've gone through
the full process

00:20:26.620 --> 00:20:30.700
of we've developed this
extension inside of RStudio

00:20:30.700 --> 00:20:33.880
or some other editor using
Plumber and plumbertableau.

00:20:33.880 --> 00:20:36.280
We've published the extension
to RStudio Connect, where

00:20:36.280 --> 00:20:39.700
it can then be accessed and
used by Tableau users who

00:20:39.700 --> 00:20:41.230
have to come in--

00:20:41.230 --> 00:20:42.940
let me come back to RStudio, OK.

00:20:42.940 --> 00:20:47.710
Come in, copy the script value,
paste it into their workbook

00:20:47.710 --> 00:20:49.780
under a calculated
field, and then supply

00:20:49.780 --> 00:20:53.805
the appropriate values to
that extension function.

00:20:53.805 --> 00:20:55.180
And in our case,
that appropriate

00:20:55.180 --> 00:20:58.060
value-- if I pull
this up one more time.

00:20:58.060 --> 00:21:00.250
An appropriate value
is this Input Text.

00:21:00.250 --> 00:21:03.920
Typically-- again, very,
very trivial example.

00:21:03.920 --> 00:21:05.710
So in practice, what
we might see here

00:21:05.710 --> 00:21:09.490
is we might see something like
multiple values being passed

00:21:09.490 --> 00:21:11.860
in from the
underlying data source

00:21:11.860 --> 00:21:14.940
that my Tableau workbook
is built on top of.

00:21:14.940 --> 00:21:16.690
And then we get really
nice functionality,

00:21:16.690 --> 00:21:20.800
where if a user comes in and
filters the data or changes

00:21:20.800 --> 00:21:22.210
the view or
otherwise manipulates

00:21:22.210 --> 00:21:25.630
the underlying data, that
triggers this extension

00:21:25.630 --> 00:21:28.070
to reevaluate with new data.

00:21:28.070 --> 00:21:30.700
So this is a very responsive
real time interaction

00:21:30.700 --> 00:21:32.698
between some third
party service.

00:21:32.698 --> 00:21:34.240
In our case, that
third party service

00:21:34.240 --> 00:21:37.810
is RStudio Connect that's
hosting this R script,

00:21:37.810 --> 00:21:39.580
this R function
that we're running,

00:21:39.580 --> 00:21:42.970
and it's responding in real time
to how users are interacting

00:21:42.970 --> 00:21:45.490
with the Tableau workbook.

00:21:45.490 --> 00:21:50.770
Just to highlight that,
let me come back here.

00:21:50.770 --> 00:21:52.178
So here's an example.

00:21:52.178 --> 00:21:53.970
Let me see if this will
pull up and render.

00:21:53.970 --> 00:21:55.470
Sometimes it's a
little bit finicky,

00:21:55.470 --> 00:21:58.570
but we'll see if we can
make something happen.

00:21:58.570 --> 00:22:02.640
So here's an example
of something that's

00:22:02.640 --> 00:22:03.900
a little bit more involved.

00:22:03.900 --> 00:22:06.600
So we have-- once
this pulls up here.

00:22:06.600 --> 00:22:09.300
We have two different-- actually
two different extensions

00:22:09.300 --> 00:22:11.008
that are being hosted
on RStudio Connect.

00:22:11.008 --> 00:22:14.400
One is an R extension that
does real time outlier

00:22:14.400 --> 00:22:16.750
detection giving us--
given a set of input data.

00:22:16.750 --> 00:22:20.552
So we consider-- this is
a transactional data set

00:22:20.552 --> 00:22:21.760
that we're working with here.

00:22:21.760 --> 00:22:25.800
So we consider sales and
profit for a given customer,

00:22:25.800 --> 00:22:27.900
and then based on
sales and profit,

00:22:27.900 --> 00:22:30.930
we determine which customers
appear to be outliers given

00:22:30.930 --> 00:22:32.820
the totality of the data set.

00:22:32.820 --> 00:22:35.820
That all happens in R. Then
we have another extension

00:22:35.820 --> 00:22:36.660
written in Python.

00:22:36.660 --> 00:22:39.780
So there's a companion
package to plumbertableau

00:22:39.780 --> 00:22:42.840
called Fast API Tableau that
does the same thing, just

00:22:42.840 --> 00:22:43.878
on the Python side.

00:22:43.878 --> 00:22:46.420
And so we have another extension
written in Python-- in fact,

00:22:46.420 --> 00:22:48.960
let me just pull that
up while that's loading.

00:22:48.960 --> 00:22:52.500
And this extension takes a
previously trained model,

00:22:52.500 --> 00:22:54.960
some input from Tableau
and uses that input

00:22:54.960 --> 00:22:57.000
to generate new
predicted values.

00:22:57.000 --> 00:22:58.305
And that allows us to do--

00:22:58.305 --> 00:23:00.180
in the case of our
workbook, what we're doing

00:23:00.180 --> 00:23:01.597
is we're saying,
OK, given what we

00:23:01.597 --> 00:23:04.410
know about this customer and
their transactional history,

00:23:04.410 --> 00:23:06.480
what amount of profit
do we predict out

00:23:06.480 --> 00:23:08.618
of this customer for
this transaction?

00:23:08.618 --> 00:23:10.410
And so we take that
predicted profit value.

00:23:10.410 --> 00:23:12.960
We compare it to the actual
profit of the transaction,

00:23:12.960 --> 00:23:15.300
and then we can identify
under and overperformers

00:23:15.300 --> 00:23:17.310
based on that
metric of comparing,

00:23:17.310 --> 00:23:19.380
predicted to realize profit.

00:23:19.380 --> 00:23:23.730
Let me just open up
that example here, OK.

00:23:23.730 --> 00:23:26.702
So this view is a little
bit different than what

00:23:26.702 --> 00:23:27.660
we had seen previously.

00:23:27.660 --> 00:23:31.965
And the reason for this is
the Fast API Tableau package

00:23:31.965 --> 00:23:34.087
has a slightly
different interface when

00:23:34.087 --> 00:23:36.420
it comes to rendering the
documentation that we generate

00:23:36.420 --> 00:23:37.920
for Tableau users,
and there will

00:23:37.920 --> 00:23:40.712
be an update that gets made to
the plumbertableau package that

00:23:40.712 --> 00:23:42.670
brings a similar style
interface there as well.

00:23:42.670 --> 00:23:45.443
So there's a more unified
feeling between the two.

00:23:45.443 --> 00:23:46.860
But we see the
same behavior here.

00:23:46.860 --> 00:23:48.150
We can come in, and
we can say using

00:23:48.150 --> 00:23:49.590
Tableau workbooks, same thing.

00:23:49.590 --> 00:23:52.472
We can Copy and Paste this value
into R or Tableau workbook.

00:23:52.472 --> 00:23:54.180
We have a description
of what we're doing

00:23:54.180 --> 00:23:56.880
and the values we're expecting.

00:23:56.880 --> 00:23:59.070
If I come back to
Tableau, here--

00:23:59.070 --> 00:24:02.580
let me see if I can
get this to work.

00:24:02.580 --> 00:24:04.950
I just want to
showcase what it might

00:24:04.950 --> 00:24:08.170
look like in a more
comprehensive example,

00:24:08.170 --> 00:24:10.890
and so we should be able to
get this to come through here

00:24:10.890 --> 00:24:11.520
in a minute.

00:24:11.520 --> 00:24:15.400
It just takes a minute to
wake Tableau up sometimes.

00:24:15.400 --> 00:24:16.890
So if I come in
here, I just want

00:24:16.890 --> 00:24:18.810
to highlight-- this is
we had-- sometimes it

00:24:18.810 --> 00:24:19.810
has a problem rendering.

00:24:19.810 --> 00:24:22.260
But if I look at
this description

00:24:22.260 --> 00:24:24.780
of this particular
extension, we can see,

00:24:24.780 --> 00:24:27.840
here, I'm referencing,
again, the location

00:24:27.840 --> 00:24:31.740
of the extension itself as it
relates to RStudio Connect,

00:24:31.740 --> 00:24:34.050
and then here are the actual
values that I'm passing in

00:24:34.050 --> 00:24:36.060
from my workbook to Tableau.

00:24:36.060 --> 00:24:39.400
So I have five different values
that I'm passing in here.

00:24:39.400 --> 00:24:42.840
These are all inputs to
the model that I trained,

00:24:42.840 --> 00:24:46.440
and then those values get
used on the Connect side

00:24:46.440 --> 00:24:50.010
or on the extension side to
generate new predicted values

00:24:50.010 --> 00:24:52.110
when it comes to
profit and return

00:24:52.110 --> 00:24:53.340
that value back into here.

00:24:56.260 --> 00:25:01.360
Yeah, and I think because
we reset the extension

00:25:01.360 --> 00:25:02.860
in the middle of
this, it timed out,

00:25:02.860 --> 00:25:05.830
so it wasn't able to
complete its trip.

00:25:05.830 --> 00:25:11.110
Anyway, so that's at a very
like I said simplistic level.

00:25:11.110 --> 00:25:14.140
That's the idea behind
plumbertableau and by extension

00:25:14.140 --> 00:25:15.070
Fast API Tableau.

00:25:15.070 --> 00:25:20.170
Is to say look, let's say that
I have some business logic,

00:25:20.170 --> 00:25:23.620
I have some complex arithmetic,
or machine learning,

00:25:23.620 --> 00:25:25.150
or whatever the
case is that I need

00:25:25.150 --> 00:25:29.800
to do as some real time
interaction with my Tableau

00:25:29.800 --> 00:25:32.140
workbook takes place, and
whatever it is that I'm trying

00:25:32.140 --> 00:25:35.740
to do extends beyond what
Tableau natively gives me

00:25:35.740 --> 00:25:36.880
in terms of capabilities.

00:25:36.880 --> 00:25:39.380
And that's often the case when
it comes to machine learning.

00:25:39.380 --> 00:25:41.630
There's some rudimentary
machine learning capabilities

00:25:41.630 --> 00:25:43.142
in Tableau, but if
I'm looking to do

00:25:43.142 --> 00:25:45.100
something a little bit
more complex or involved

00:25:45.100 --> 00:25:46.660
or something like
outlier detection, which

00:25:46.660 --> 00:25:48.243
is one of the examples
that we provide

00:25:48.243 --> 00:25:51.400
in our online
repository, then this

00:25:51.400 --> 00:25:53.290
becomes a really
compelling use case

00:25:53.290 --> 00:25:55.390
because I can
essentially say I'm

00:25:55.390 --> 00:25:57.430
going to take the data
that I have in Tableau,

00:25:57.430 --> 00:25:59.388
I'm going to send it off
somewhere or something

00:25:59.388 --> 00:26:01.930
to be done to it, and then I'm
going to get the results back.

00:26:01.930 --> 00:26:04.870
And in this case, we've shown
I can send that data off

00:26:04.870 --> 00:26:06.040
to RStudio Connect.

00:26:06.040 --> 00:26:09.010
This extension can execute and
then return the results back

00:26:09.010 --> 00:26:10.810
into Tableau, and
I can then further

00:26:10.810 --> 00:26:14.980
use those results to
improve my analysis

00:26:14.980 --> 00:26:17.920
or enhance my
visualization, or whatever

00:26:17.920 --> 00:26:20.855
I'm doing with the results once
they come back is up to me.

00:26:20.855 --> 00:26:23.230
There's a few caveats that I
think are worth putting out.

00:26:23.230 --> 00:26:26.830
One is Tableau is
interesting in the sense

00:26:26.830 --> 00:26:28.750
that if I'm passing in--

00:26:28.750 --> 00:26:30.910
this is a pretty, again,
trivial too example,

00:26:30.910 --> 00:26:34.180
but if I'm referencing a
large underlying data set

00:26:34.180 --> 00:26:37.780
and I'm trying to pass that data
set in bulk to my extension,

00:26:37.780 --> 00:26:41.170
it depends on how I'm actually
calculating my calculated field

00:26:41.170 --> 00:26:44.380
inside of Tableau, because
Tableau will either

00:26:44.380 --> 00:26:46.780
send one request
with all the data,

00:26:46.780 --> 00:26:49.300
or it will send one request
per observation, which can

00:26:49.300 --> 00:26:50.660
dramatically slow things down.

00:26:50.660 --> 00:26:53.770
So imagine I've got
100,000 rows of data.

00:26:53.770 --> 00:26:57.077
I could-- and accidentally
if I haven't set things

00:26:57.077 --> 00:26:58.660
up the right way, I
could accidentally

00:26:58.660 --> 00:27:01.060
send up sending 100,000
requests every time I

00:27:01.060 --> 00:27:03.408
try to use the extension,
and that's certainly

00:27:03.408 --> 00:27:05.950
going to be much less efficient
than sending just one request

00:27:05.950 --> 00:27:07.790
with 100,000 observations.

00:27:07.790 --> 00:27:10.000
And so we have some
documentation around things

00:27:10.000 --> 00:27:13.683
to look into and how to go
about reasoning around how

00:27:13.683 --> 00:27:15.100
to set up Tableau
so that it sends

00:27:15.100 --> 00:27:17.920
a request along the
appropriate dimension

00:27:17.920 --> 00:27:21.268
so that you end up working
as efficiently as possible.

00:27:21.268 --> 00:27:22.810
The one other thing
that I'll mention

00:27:22.810 --> 00:27:25.330
that we haven't discussed
so far is how do we

00:27:25.330 --> 00:27:27.640
set these things up from--

00:27:27.640 --> 00:27:30.460
how do we set up the connection
between Tableau and RStudio

00:27:30.460 --> 00:27:33.580
Connect, and that's a
little bit beyond the scope

00:27:33.580 --> 00:27:35.560
of plumbertableau
itself, but I think

00:27:35.560 --> 00:27:38.180
it's worth just
understanding how that works.

00:27:38.180 --> 00:27:40.900
So if I come back to the
Home page of Tableau Online

00:27:40.900 --> 00:27:44.800
and come to Settings, as
an admin in Tableau Online,

00:27:44.800 --> 00:27:47.290
if I come over to
Extensions, here's

00:27:47.290 --> 00:27:49.160
where I can do a
couple of things.

00:27:49.160 --> 00:27:51.010
One, I can enable
Analytics Extensions.

00:27:51.010 --> 00:27:54.310
So about halfway down the page,
there's Analytics Extensions.

00:27:54.310 --> 00:27:56.440
I would choose to enable
those for my site.

00:27:56.440 --> 00:27:59.260
And then there isn't
anything pre-configured,

00:27:59.260 --> 00:28:02.710
so I need to create a new
connection from this option

00:28:02.710 --> 00:28:04.390
list, also like
analytics extensions

00:28:04.390 --> 00:28:05.920
API, which is the final option.

00:28:05.920 --> 00:28:08.170
And then in here, we can
say-- we can give it a name--

00:28:08.170 --> 00:28:10.060
RStudio Connect.

00:28:10.060 --> 00:28:13.330
Provide the hostname--
myrstudioconnect.com,

00:28:13.330 --> 00:28:15.730
and the port, whatever
that might be.

00:28:15.730 --> 00:28:18.520
The username is always
rstudio-connect,

00:28:18.520 --> 00:28:22.330
and then the password here is
any valid RStudio Connect User

00:28:22.330 --> 00:28:24.010
API Key.

00:28:24.010 --> 00:28:26.860
And the thing to understand
about this setup-- again, two

00:28:26.860 --> 00:28:28.360
things.

00:28:28.360 --> 00:28:31.690
One, this is managed and done
by the Tableau administrator,

00:28:31.690 --> 00:28:33.687
so individual users aren't
managing connections

00:28:33.687 --> 00:28:34.270
at this level.

00:28:34.270 --> 00:28:35.978
This is done at the
administrative level.

00:28:35.978 --> 00:28:38.110
And then when you create
a workbook as a user,

00:28:38.110 --> 00:28:40.540
you can select which connection
you want to actually use

00:28:40.540 --> 00:28:41.890
from that workbook.

00:28:41.890 --> 00:28:44.430
So it's done by
the administrator.

00:28:44.430 --> 00:28:47.450
The other thing is this
password is centrally used.

00:28:47.450 --> 00:28:50.900
And what I mean by that is
whatever API key is input here

00:28:50.900 --> 00:28:53.180
by the Tableau admin
is the key that

00:28:53.180 --> 00:28:57.090
will be used any time an
extension uses this connection.

00:28:57.090 --> 00:28:59.780
So if this key is
my own personal key,

00:28:59.780 --> 00:29:01.640
that means my personal
RStudio Connect

00:29:01.640 --> 00:29:04.310
user has to be added
to every extension that

00:29:04.310 --> 00:29:07.880
is published in order for
it to be used from Tableau.

00:29:07.880 --> 00:29:09.830
Our recommended approach
here is to create

00:29:09.830 --> 00:29:14.210
a service account called
Tableau or Tableau Extension.

00:29:14.210 --> 00:29:15.740
Really call it
whatever you want.

00:29:15.740 --> 00:29:20.030
But then use an API key from
that service account as the key

00:29:20.030 --> 00:29:23.270
that you provide here
in the setup on Tableau,

00:29:23.270 --> 00:29:25.730
and then add that Tableau user--

00:29:25.730 --> 00:29:27.620
again, whatever you
happen to have called

00:29:27.620 --> 00:29:28.580
that service account.

00:29:28.580 --> 00:29:30.350
Add that user to
every extension that's

00:29:30.350 --> 00:29:32.730
published so that they have--

00:29:32.730 --> 00:29:36.670
so that Tableau has the ability
to call out to that extension.

00:29:36.670 --> 00:29:40.780
OK, and I think like--
again, trivial example

00:29:40.780 --> 00:29:43.150
in terms of what we're
actually doing but

00:29:43.150 --> 00:29:46.420
hopefully highlights
the flexibility

00:29:46.420 --> 00:29:48.130
of this approach
and the way in which

00:29:48.130 --> 00:29:50.560
it can be used to extend
the functionality of Tableau

00:29:50.560 --> 00:29:52.750
beyond just what's
already there.

00:29:52.750 --> 00:29:57.390
Anyway, that's
plumbertableau in a nutshell.

